<h1>Breaking Privacy in Federated Learning</h1>
<div class="small">IA-1 &middot; Problem Formulation and Preliminary Implementation &middot; Suyash Humne (16014223085)</div>

<div class="abstract">
<b>Abstract.</b> Federated learning (FL) is widely promoted on the premise that
"data never leaves the device" &mdash; participants share only model gradients,
not their raw data. This project interrogates that premise directly. We
implement a <b>gradient-inversion attack</b> in which an honest-but-curious
server reconstructs a client's private training image, pixel-by-pixel, from the
single gradient the client uploads. Using the cosine-similarity gradient-matching
method of Geiping&nbsp;et&nbsp;al. (2020) with total-variation regularisation and
analytic label recovery (iDLG), our attacker {{HERO_LABEL_OK}} recovers the
private label and reconstructs the image at <b>{{HERO_PSNR}}&nbsp;dB PSNR</b>
from only {{HERO_FLOATS}} gradient values, with no access to the pixels. The
attack is the lens through which the five course modules are unified: it
motivates and is measured against the privacy defences of Differential Privacy
(Module&nbsp;2), Local Differential Privacy (Module&nbsp;3), Privacy-Preserving
Data Mining / anonymity (Module&nbsp;4) and Compressive Privacy (Module&nbsp;5),
with Module&nbsp;1 providing the threat model. This IA-1 report establishes the
problem, maps it to all five modules, prepares the dataset, and demonstrates the
working attack as the preliminary implementation; IA-2 completes the defence
comparison and evaluation.
</div>

<h2>1&nbsp;&nbsp;Problem Definition and Significance</h2>

<h3>1.1&nbsp;&nbsp;Context and motivation</h3>
<p>Machine learning increasingly trains on data that is legally and ethically
sensitive &mdash; medical records, financial histories, biometric images.
<b>Federated learning</b> (McMahan et&nbsp;al., 2017) was introduced precisely to
make such training privacy-respecting: instead of centralising raw data, each
participant (a hospital, a phone, a bank) keeps its data locally, computes the
gradient of the shared model's loss on that local data, and transmits only the
gradient to a central server, which aggregates the gradients to update the global
model. The marketing claim is simple and appealing: <i>your data never leaves
your device.</i></p>
<p>The security question this project asks is equally simple: <b>is a gradient
actually safe to share?</b> A gradient is a deterministic function of the private
data and the known model parameters. If that function is even partially
invertible, then "sharing a gradient" leaks "sharing the data" &mdash; and the
entire privacy argument for federated learning collapses. This is not a
hypothetical: the 2019&ndash;2022 literature on <i>deep leakage from gradients</i>
established that gradients can be inverted to recover training inputs with
alarming fidelity.</p>

<h3>1.2&nbsp;&nbsp;Problem statement</h3>
<div class="callout">
Given a neural network with known architecture and parameters, and the gradient
of its loss computed on a client's single private training example, can an
honest-but-curious server <b>reconstruct that private example</b> (its pixels and
its label) without ever observing the data &mdash; and which privacy mechanisms
from the course syllabus can measurably prevent or degrade this reconstruction,
and at what cost to model utility?
</div>

<h3>1.3&nbsp;&nbsp;Objectives</h3>
<ol>
<li><b>Attack.</b> Implement a working gradient-inversion attack that reconstructs
a private image and recovers its label from a shared gradient, in a simulated
federated-learning setting.</li>
<li><b>Quantify.</b> Measure reconstruction fidelity with standard image-quality
metrics (MSE, PSNR, SSIM, LPIPS) and extend the attack from a single image to
small batches to map its limits.</li>
<li><b>Defend.</b> Implement and evaluate four defence families drawn from the
five syllabus modules &mdash; differential privacy, local differential privacy,
compressive (sparsification / quantization) privacy, and anonymity-based
aggregation &mdash; against the same attack.</li>
<li><b>Trade-off.</b> Characterise the privacy&ndash;utility trade-off of each
defence: how much reconstruction quality falls versus how much model accuracy is
sacrificed.</li>
<li><b>Corroborate.</b> Add a second, complementary privacy attack
(<b>membership inference</b>, LiRA) to measure leakage quantitatively and to
audit the differential-privacy guarantee empirically.</li>
</ol>

<h3>1.4&nbsp;&nbsp;Scope and expected outcome</h3>
<p>The scope is the <i>honest-but-curious server</i> threat model on image data
(CIFAR-10 as a public, reproducible stand-in for private image data such as
medical scans). Malicious-server attacks that modify the model architecture
(e.g. <i>Robbing the Fed</i>) are discussed analytically and used for
cross-validation but are out of the implementation scope for IA-1. The expected
outcome is a reproducible benchmark showing (a)&nbsp;that gradient sharing leaks
private data, and (b)&nbsp;a ranked, measured comparison of the syllabus defences
against that leak. This is, to our knowledge, not available as a single unified
study across all four defence families under one attack &mdash; which is the
individual research contribution (Section&nbsp;7).</p>

