<h2 class="sec-break">3&nbsp;&nbsp;Conceptual Understanding and Methodology</h2>

<h3>3.1&nbsp;&nbsp;Why a gradient leaks data</h3>
<p>For a model with parameters &theta; and loss <i>L</i>, a client holding a
private example (<i>x</i>,&nbsp;<i>y</i>) uploads the gradient
<i>g</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;&nabla;<sub>&theta;</sub><i>L</i>(<i>f</i><sub>&theta;</sub>(<i>x</i>),&nbsp;<i>y</i>).
The attacker knows &theta;, <i>f</i> and <i>L</i>, and receives <i>g</i>. It
reconstructs the input by searching for a synthetic image
<i>x&#770;</i> whose gradient <i>g&#770;</i> best matches the observed <i>g</i>:</p>
<div class="eq">x&#770;* = arg min<sub>x&#770;</sub> &nbsp; D(&nabla;<sub>&theta;</sub>L(f<sub>&theta;</sub>(x&#770;), y&#770;),&nbsp; g) &nbsp;+&nbsp; &alpha;&middot;TV(x&#770;)</div>
<p>Intuitively, the gradient encodes "how each pixel of the input would need to
change to reduce the loss", which is a rich, differentiable fingerprint of the
input itself. The reconstruction is an optimisation <i>over the input</i> while
the network weights are held fixed.</p>

<h3>3.2&nbsp;&nbsp;Design choices (and why)</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Direction, not magnitude &mdash; cosine loss.</b> We match gradients by
<i>negative cosine similarity</i>, D(&middot;)&nbsp;=&nbsp;1&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;cos(g&#770;,&nbsp;g),
rather than Euclidean distance. Cosine matching is magnitude-invariant and far
more stable than the original DLG L<sub>2</sub> objective, especially as the
model trains and gradient norms shrink (Geiping et&nbsp;al., 2020).</li>
<li><b>Total-variation prior.</b> We add &alpha;&middot;TV(<i>x&#770;</i>), the
anisotropic total variation of the candidate, which penalises high-frequency
noise and pushes the reconstruction toward the piecewise-smooth statistics of
natural images.</li>
<li><b>Analytic label recovery (iDLG).</b> The ground-truth label need not be
optimised: for single-sample cross-entropy, the class whose row of the final
layer's weight-gradient has negative sign is the true label. We read it off
directly, which removes a major source of instability.</li>
<li><b>Smooth activations, untrained network.</b> A small LeNet with
<i>Sigmoid</i> activations (not ReLU) and a freshly initialised (untrained)
network leak the cleanest reconstructions &mdash; the easiest, clearest regime
for the demonstration, and the honest worst case for the defender.</li>
<li><b>Restarts + group consistency.</b> Reconstruction is seed-sensitive, so we
run several random restarts and take a pixel-wise consensus (median) over
candidates &mdash; the near-free quality trick from GradInversion.</li>
</ul>

